# The ledger of `ui`: what a program with windows of the system's own
# widgets can do, one block per capability, and what Rebel answers. The
# lists are this machine's, 2026-11-22. Go's standard library holds no user
# interface, so its column is `github.com/andlabs/ui`
# v0.0.0-20200610043537-70a69d6ae31e (libui: each system's own widgets, the
# manner this package takes), read by `go doc -all`. Python's is `tkinter`, its
# standard library's, with `tkinter.ttk`, `messagebox`, `filedialog`,
# `simpledialog`, `colorchooser`, `font`, `dnd` and `scrolledtext`, its
# public classes and functions listed from the modules' declarations by
# `ast`, since this Python 3.15.2 is built without `_tkinter` and `dir()`
# cannot import it; a method is named after its class. Rust's standard
# library holds no user interface and no crate of each system's own widgets
# is Rust's common one, so its column is `-` but where Tauri 2.12.5, which
# this machine holds and whose public structs were looked up in it, names
# the same thing: the web view and the tray. A block's `wx` line names the
# classes and members of wxWidgets 3.2, the library whose manner this package
# takes, as its reference lists them (https://docs.wxwidgets.org/3.2/, the
# class list and each class's page, read 2026-11-22).

capability ui/app  a user interface opened on the system's own widgets or with no screen, and closed
  heroes  ui.open, ui.close, ui.is_native, ui.App, ui.Backend
  go      ui.Main, ui.Quit, ui.OnShouldQuit
  rust    -
  python  tkinter.Tk, tkinter.NoDefaultRoot
  wx      wxApp (OnInit, OnExit, ExitMainLoop), wxAppConsole
  note    an app is a handle the program closes, told when `main` returns where it did not (spec § 13); `ui.Main` runs the program inside the system's loop and `tkinter.mainloop` never returns, where here the program asks for each event

capability ui/events  what the person did, asked for and waited on for a bounded time
  heroes  ui.wait, ui.Event
  go      Button.OnClicked, Checkbox.OnToggled, Combobox.OnSelected, Entry.OnChanged, MultilineEntry.OnChanged, RadioButtons.OnSelected, Slider.OnChanged, Window.OnClosing
  rust    -
  python  tkinter.mainloop, tkinter.Event, tkinter.EventType, tkinter.Misc.bind
  wx      wxEvtHandler (Bind), wxEventLoopBase (Run, Pending, Dispatch, DispatchTimeout)
  note    the three libraries call the program back from the system's loop; the language has no closures, so `wait` answers a variant naming the widget by the handle the program holds, and the program matches it (README § B3)

capability ui/inject  what a person does, given by the program, to drive a test or a headless demo
  heroes  ui.inject, ui.Input
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  tkinter.Misc.event_generate
  wx      wxUIActionSimulator (MouseClick, Char, Text, Select)
  note    on the native side each input goes through the system's own path (`performClick` on macOS), so its event comes as the person's would

capability ui/keys-pressed  a key pressed by the program as the person's keyboard presses it, through the system's own path
  heroes  ui.press_key
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  wx      wxUIActionSimulator (KeyDown, KeyUp, Char)
  note    native on macOS (the key goes down and up through `NSApp.sendEvent`, the window made the key window of the active application first), Windows by `SendInput` (the window brought to the foreground first, the foreground lock's refusal told as `failed`, and the loop run until the key's release comes back), Linux by X11's XTest extension (`libXtst.so.6`, opened where installed; the window given the X server's focus first), refused on Wayland, which lets no program press a key; on the headless side a key chooses the menu item whose shortcut it is

capability ui/edit-menu  the system's Edit menu in every text widget: undo, redo, cut, copy, paste, delete and select all, by the mouse and by their keys
  heroes  ui.text_field, ui.text_area
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  wx      the stock identifiers wxID_UNDO, wxID_REDO, wxID_CUT, wxID_COPY, wxID_PASTE, wxID_DELETE, wxID_SELECTALL
  note    on macOS the arm's menu bar holds the standard Edit menu after the window's first menu, each item sending AppKit's own action to the widget with the keys (without it Command+C and the rest reached no text widget); Windows' edit controls take Ctrl+C and the rest themselves, and Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+Y and Alt+Backspace answer from the arm's own undo (ui/text-selection), and GTK's `GtkText` and `GtkTextView` likewise, measured: Ctrl+Z, Shift+Ctrl+Z and Ctrl+A in a field and a text area under Xvfb

capability ui/headless  the widgets kept and laid out with no screen, and drawn as text
  heroes  ui.render, ui.Backend
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  wx      - (every port of wxWidgets draws on a display; none keeps its widgets as data with no screen)
  note    every machine draws the same lines, so a demo's transcript is a test

capability ui/native-windows  the native side on Windows, by Win32
  heroes  ui.open, ui.is_native, ui.Backend
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  wx      wxWidgets' wxMSW port
  note    Win32's own controls, aware of each monitor's scale and drawn in the common controls' current look; libui's Windows side draws Win32's controls too, under the same `ui.Main`, and tkinter draws Tk's own

capability ui/native-linux  the native side on Linux and every other system, by GTK 4
  heroes  ui.open, ui.is_native, ui.Backend
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  wx      wxWidgets' wxGTK port
  note    GTK 4 opened at the first call with `dlopen`, so no build needs it and a machine without `libgtk-4.so.1` or a display opens headless under `.automatic`; libui's Unix side is GTK 3, and tkinter draws Tk's own widgets

capability ui/webview  the system's web view as a widget, with a bridge to its scripts
  heroes  ui/webview.webview, ui/webview.post, ui.Kind, ui.Event, ui.Input
  go      -
  rust    tauri::WebviewWindow, tauri::Webview
  python  -
  wx      wxWebView (RunScriptAsync, AddScriptMessageHandler, RegisterHandler), wxWebViewHandler
  note    the web view, its assets, its policy and its bridge are `ui/webview`'s, a subpackage so only the programs that show a page carry its rules, and `ui/webview.ledger` holds each capability; this package's `ui.Kind.webview`, `ui.Event.message` and `ui.Input.send` are what its app reads and gives of them

capability ui/mailbox  a thread's message to the user interface, and a poll set the wait watches
  heroes  ui.add_mailbox, ui.add_set, ui.remove_source, ui.Event.ready
  go      ui.QueueMain
  rust    -
  python  -
  wx      wxThreadEvent, wxEvtHandler (QueueEvent, CallAfter)
  note    panel 256's R9: another thread's work reaches the user interface as a value sent to a mailbox the app's wait watches, ending `wait` as `.ready` at once (feature 2647's mailbox, the set's own doorbell: no lock is taken by the send); a program that waits on sockets, streams and timers beside its windows has `wait` watch their `poll.Set` the same way, a timer cutting the wait short; the program then takes what is ready itself (`poll.wait` of 0, `receive` within 0), an entry that stays ready told again at the next wait and never twice within one; macOS by a `CFFileDescriptor` on the set's `kqueue` in the main run loop; Linux pending (`g_unix_fd_add` on its `epoll`); Windows pending (a Windows set is `WSAPoll` over an array and holds no handle to wait on; the likeliest way is one event the set owns, `WSAEventSelect` per socket, unpriced), answering `unsupported` meanwhile, as the headless side does (its app has no wait on the system)

capability ui/window  a window: its title, its size, whether the person may resize it, and its close button asked
  heroes  ui.window, ui.set_text, ui.set_size, ui.set_resizable, ui.rect_of, ui.Event.close_asked
  go      ui.NewWindow, Window.SetTitle, Window.Title, Window.SetChild, Window.OnClosing
  rust    -
  python  tkinter.Toplevel, tkinter.Wm
  wx      wxFrame, wxTopLevelWindow (SetTitle), wxWindow (SetClientSize, SetMinClientSize, GetClientRect), wxCloseEvent
  note    a window lays its children out in a column, so it needs no child of its own, `Window.SetChild`'s

capability ui/screens  the screens of the system: each one's whole area, its work area and its scale, the main one first
  heroes  ui.screens, ui.Screen
  go      -
  rust    tauri::Monitor, tauri::Window::available_monitors, tauri::Window::primary_monitor, tauri::Window::current_monitor
  python  tkinter.Misc.winfo_screenwidth, tkinter.Misc.winfo_screenheight, tkinter.Misc.winfo_vrootwidth, tkinter.Misc.winfo_vrootheight
  wx      wxDisplay (GetCount, GetGeometry, GetClientArea, GetScaleFactor, IsPrimary)
  note    in points from the main screen's top-left corner, the y axis down, the scale in hundredths (200 on a Retina screen); macOS by `NSScreen`, Windows by `EnumDisplayMonitors`, `GetMonitorInfoW` and `GetDpiForMonitor`, each monitor in points at its own scale and its origin at the primary's, Linux by `GdkDisplay`'s monitors, X11's primary one first, the work area the X11 backend's (`_NET_WORKAREA`, a window manager's) and the whole area where none is set; the headless side has one screen of 1920 by 1080 points

capability ui/window-position  where a window stands on a screen, set and read, centred on its screen or over another window, and its moves told
  heroes  ui.set_position, ui.frame_of, ui.center, ui.center_over, ui.Frame, ui.Event.moved, ui.Event.screen_changed, ui.Input.drag_window
  go      -
  rust    tauri::Window::set_position, tauri::Window::outer_position, tauri::Window::center, tauri::WindowEvent::Moved
  python  tkinter.Wm.wm_geometry, tkinter.Misc.winfo_x, tkinter.Misc.winfo_y, tkinter.Misc.winfo_rootx, tkinter.Misc.winfo_rooty
  wx      wxTopLevelWindow (SetPosition, GetPosition, GetScreenPosition, Centre, CentreOnParent, CentreOnScreen), wxMoveEvent
  note    a position is the frame's top-left corner, its title bar included, from the top-left corner of the screen it is on; macOS (the arm keeps a window's top-left corner where its layout resizes it), Windows by `SetWindowPos` and `MonitorFromWindow` on `GetWindowRect`'s frame, the border Windows keeps for resizing included, a minimized or maximized window's restored frame through its placement, Linux by the X server on X11 (`XMoveWindow` and `XTranslateCoordinates` on the window's X window, a window manager's frame read from `_NET_FRAME_EXTENTS` and its gravity learned from its first move), refused on Wayland, which lets no program place or read a toplevel (`xdg_toplevel` has no such request), the face answering `unsupported` there

capability ui/window-state  a window minimized, maximized, in full screen or restored, asked which, and the person's change told
  heroes  ui.set_state, ui.state_of, ui.WindowState, ui.Event.state_changed, ui.Input.change_state
  go      -
  rust    tauri::Window::minimize, tauri::Window::maximize, tauri::Window::unmaximize, tauri::Window::unminimize, tauri::Window::set_fullscreen, tauri::Window::is_minimized, tauri::Window::is_maximized, tauri::Window::is_fullscreen
  python  tkinter.Wm.wm_state, tkinter.Wm.wm_iconify, tkinter.Wm.wm_deiconify, tkinter.Wm.wm_attributes
  wx      wxTopLevelWindow (Iconize, Maximize, Restore, ShowFullScreen, IsIconized, IsMaximized, IsFullScreen), wxIconizeEvent, wxMaximizeEvent, wxFullScreenEvent
  note    the call returns once the system has made the state, its animation included; the new size comes as `.resized`; macOS (`miniaturize`, `zoom`, `toggleFullScreen`), Windows by `ShowWindow`, `IsIconic` and `IsZoomed`, full screen a frameless window over its monitor with its style and placement kept, the person's states through the title bar's `WM_SYSCOMMAND`, Linux by `gtk_window_minimize` and the rest, read from `GdkToplevelState`, where X11 leaves a state to a window manager, so with none running (`_NET_SUPPORTING_WM_CHECK`) the face answers `unsupported`, and Wayland lets no program read or undo a minimized state and the face answers `unsupported`

capability ui/window-look  a window kept above every other, without its frame, see-through, or on the system's translucent material
  heroes  ui.set_on_top, ui.set_framed, ui.set_opacity, ui.set_backdrop, ui.Backdrop
  go      Window.Borderless, Window.SetBorderless
  rust    tauri::Window::set_always_on_top, tauri::Window::set_decorations, tauri::window::Effect, tauri::Window::set_effects
  python  tkinter.Wm.wm_attributes, tkinter.Wm.wm_overrideredirect
  wx      wxFrame (the wxSTAY_ON_TOP style), wxWindow (the wxBORDER_NONE style), wxTopLevelWindow (SetTransparent, CanSetTransparent)
  note    a window with no frame is moved by a drag on its background (on Windows its content alone, no sizing border, resized by its content's edges where it may be); the material is `NSVisualEffectView` behind the window on macOS, Mica on Windows 11 (`DwmSetWindowAttribute`'s system backdrop, `unsupported` before Windows 11 22H2), and none in GTK 4, which answers `unsupported`; on Linux the frame by `gtk_window_set_decorated`, the opacity by `gtk_widget_set_opacity`, and always on top by EWMH's `_NET_WM_STATE_ABOVE` on X11 where a window manager offers it, refused on Wayland, which has no such level; libui's borderless window, dropped here until this block, is `set_framed`

capability ui/app-icon  the application's icon, set at run time
  heroes  ui.set_icon
  go      -
  rust    tauri::Window::set_icon
  python  tkinter.Wm.wm_iconphoto, tkinter.Wm.wm_iconbitmap
  wx      wxTopLevelWindow (SetIcon, SetIcons)
  note    the Dock's on macOS, every window's and the task bar's on Windows (pending, `WM_SETICON`); every window's on Linux by `gdk_toplevel_set_icon_list`, X11's `_NET_WM_ICON` (GTK 4's window takes an icon by its theme name, its surface from pixels)

capability ui/modal  a window of the program's own that the person answers before any other
  heroes  ui.set_modal
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  tkinter.Misc.grab_set, tkinter.Misc.grab_release
  wx      wxDialog (ShowModal, EndModal, IsModal)
  note    the program goes on asking `wait`, no loop of the system's runs in its place; macOS by an `NSApp` modal session run in place of the system's loop, Windows by `EnableWindow` on every other window of the app, and on each one made while it stands, Linux by `gtk_window_set_modal`, transient for another window of the app's, GTK's grab keeping the others from the person's acts

capability ui/boxes  widgets side by side and one under another, with spacing, padding, and the room left shared by weights
  heroes  ui.row, ui.column, ui.set_spacing, ui.set_fit, ui.default_fit, ui.Fit, ui.Align
  go      ui.NewHorizontalBox, ui.NewVerticalBox, Box.Append, Box.Delete, Box.Padded, Box.SetPadded, ui.Align
  rust    -
  python  tkinter.Pack, tkinter.Place, tkinter.Frame, ttk.Frame
  wx      wxBoxSizer, wxSizer (Add, AddSpacer), wxSizerFlags (Proportion, Expand, Border, Align)
  note    the layout is computed by Rebel the same on every system, in wxWidgets' sizers' manner; a widget's place given by hand, `tkinter.Place`'s, is refused: it is what a layout is for

capability ui/grid  widgets in cells of columns and rows, the room left shared by the columns' and rows' weights
  heroes  ui.grid, ui.set_grid_stretch, ui.set_span
  go      ui.NewGrid, Grid.Append, Grid.InsertAt, Grid.Padded, Grid.SetPadded, ui.At
  rust    -
  python  tkinter.Grid
  wx      wxFlexGridSizer (AddGrowableCol, AddGrowableRow), wxGridSizer, wxGridBagSizer, wxGBSpan
  note    catalogue LY03; a child takes columns from its cell rightwards, cells filling row after row and a child that does not fit in what is left of its row starting the next, its columns widened, the last most, where together narrower than it needs; computed by this package, so every system lays it out alike; a cell spanning rows, and a child placed at a cell of the program's choosing (`wxGridBagSizer`'s position), are not offered

capability ui/form  labelled fields, a label and its field on each line
  heroes  ui.grid
  go      ui.NewForm, Form.Append, Form.Delete, Form.Padded, Form.SetPadded
  rust    -
  python  -
  wx      wxFlexGridSizer
  note    a form is a grid of two columns, labels in the first

capability ui/group  a box drawn with a title and a frame around it
  heroes  ui.group, ui.Kind.group
  go      ui.NewGroup, Group.SetChild, Group.SetTitle, Group.Title, Group.Margined, Group.SetMargined
  rust    -
  python  tkinter.LabelFrame, ttk.Labelframe
  wx      wxStaticBox, wxStaticBoxSizer
  note    catalogue LY11; a frame as a page is: its widgets are made in it, laid out as a column in its own content from the room its frame and title leave (read from the system on the native side, `sizes.GROUP_SIDE` and `GROUP_TOP` on the headless side) and a padding inside it, its title `set_text`'s and `text_of`'s; macOS by an `NSBox` with a flipped content view; Windows pending (`BS_GROUPBOX` draws the frame and title and holds nothing, so a child window over its inner rectangle holds the widgets), Linux pending (`GtkFrame` with a `GtkFixed` child)

capability ui/label  a text the person reads
  heroes  ui.label
  go      ui.NewLabel, Label.SetText, Label.Text
  rust    -
  python  tkinter.Label, ttk.Label, tkinter.Message
  wx      wxStaticText

capability ui/button  a button the person presses
  heroes  ui.button, ui.Event.clicked
  go      ui.NewButton, Button.SetText, Button.Text, Button.OnClicked
  rust    -
  python  tkinter.Button, ttk.Button
  wx      wxButton (wxEVT_BUTTON)

capability ui/button-keys  the buttons Return and Escape press wherever their window has the keys: a dialog's OK, drawn as the default, and its Cancel
  heroes  ui.set_button_key, ui.ButtonKey
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  tkinter.Button
  wx      wxButton (SetDefault), wxDialog (SetAffirmativeId, SetEscapeId)
  note    tkinter's `default` option of a button draws it and binds no key; a window's button for Return takes Return before its text fields, as each system's dialogs do; macOS by `defaultButtonCell` and the Escape key equivalent, Windows by the dialog manager's `IDOK` and `IDCANCEL` (`IsDialogMessageW`), the button for Return drawn `BS_DEFPUSHBUTTON`, Linux by `gtk_window_set_default_widget` and a field's Return handed to that button, Escape a shortcut of the window's activating its button

capability ui/text-field  one line of text the person types, set and read back
  heroes  ui.text_field, ui.set_text, ui.text_of, ui.Event.text_changed, ui.Event.submitted, ui.Input.submit
  go      ui.NewEntry, Entry.SetText, Entry.Text, Entry.OnChanged, Entry.ReadOnly, Entry.SetReadOnly
  rust    -
  python  tkinter.Entry, ttk.Entry
  wx      wxTextCtrl (wxTE_PROCESS_ENTER, wxEVT_TEXT_ENTER)
  note    a field the person may not change is one greyed out, `set_enabled`; Return pressed in it is `.submitted`, unless its window's button for Return takes it (macOS by the field's action, Linux by `GtkText`'s `activate`, Windows pending: `EN_` has none, so a subclass's `WM_KEYDOWN` `VK_RETURN`)

capability ui/text-area  lines of text the person types
  heroes  ui.text_area, ui.set_text, ui.text_of
  go      ui.NewMultilineEntry, ui.NewNonWrappingMultilineEntry, MultilineEntry.Append, MultilineEntry.SetText, MultilineEntry.Text, MultilineEntry.OnChanged, MultilineEntry.ReadOnly, MultilineEntry.SetReadOnly
  rust    -
  python  tkinter.Text, scrolledtext.ScrolledText
  wx      wxTextCtrl (wxTE_MULTILINE)
  note    text is added by setting the whole text, `Append`'s

capability ui/password  a text field whose text is masked
  heroes  ui.password_field
  go      ui.NewPasswordEntry
  rust    -
  python  -
  wx      wxTextCtrl (wxTE_PASSWORD)
  note    a text field in every other way; `render` draws one mark a character and never the text; macOS by `NSSecureTextField` put in the field's place, Windows by `EM_SETPASSWORDCHAR`, Linux by a `GtkPasswordEntry` put in the field's place

capability ui/checkbox  a box the person checks and unchecks
  heroes  ui.checkbox, ui.set_checked, ui.is_checked, ui.Event.toggled
  go      ui.NewCheckbox, Checkbox.Checked, Checkbox.SetChecked, Checkbox.SetText, Checkbox.Text, Checkbox.OnToggled
  rust    -
  python  tkinter.Checkbutton, ttk.Checkbutton
  wx      wxCheckBox (wxEVT_CHECKBOX)

capability ui/radio  items of which the person chooses one, each with its round button
  heroes  ui.radio_group, ui.set_selection, ui.selection_of
  go      ui.NewRadioButtons, RadioButtons.Append, RadioButtons.Selected, RadioButtons.SetSelected, RadioButtons.OnSelected
  rust    -
  python  tkinter.Radiobutton, ttk.Radiobutton
  wx      wxRadioBox, wxRadioButton

capability ui/list  items in a box, of which the person chooses one or several
  heroes  ui.list, ui.set_items, ui.set_selection, ui.selection_of, ui.Event.selection_changed, ui.Event.activated, ui.Input.activate
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  tkinter.Listbox
  wx      wxListBox (wxEVT_LISTBOX_DCLICK), wxListCtrl (wxEVT_LIST_ITEM_ACTIVATED)
  note    a row opened by a double click or the Return key is `.activated`, after the `.selection_changed` its first click gave (feature 2685); tkinter binds `<Double-Button-1>` itself and libui has no list; macOS by the table's double action and its Return key, Windows by `LBN_DBLCLK`, and Return through the dialog manager's `IDOK` opening the first row chosen, Linux by `GtkListBox`'s `row-activated`, single-click activation off

capability ui/combo  items in a button that opens them, of which the person chooses one
  heroes  ui.combo, ui.set_items, ui.set_selection, ui.selection_of
  go      ui.NewCombobox, Combobox.Append, Combobox.Selected, Combobox.SetSelected, Combobox.OnSelected
  rust    -
  python  ttk.Combobox, tkinter.OptionMenu, ttk.OptionMenu
  wx      wxChoice

capability ui/editable-combo  a combo box the person may type into
  heroes  ui.editable_combo, ui.Kind.editable_combo
  go      ui.NewEditableCombobox, EditableCombobox.Append, EditableCombobox.SetText, EditableCombobox.Text, EditableCombobox.OnChanged
  rust    -
  python  ttk.Combobox
  wx      wxComboBox
  note    catalogue CT28; a field with a list of items beside it, the items `set_items`'s (the text kept) and the text `set_text`'s and `text_of`'s; typing is `.text_changed`, choosing an item makes it the text and is `.selection_changed` with its index and then `.text_changed`; the choice read is the first item equal to the text, or none, and `set_selection` takes one index and types that item; macOS by an `NSComboBox` with no data source and completion on; Windows pending (`CBS_DROPDOWN`, `CBN_EDITCHANGE` and `CBN_SELCHANGE`), Linux has no widget for it (`GtkDropDown` only searches its items, `GtkComboBox` and `GtkEntryCompletion` are deprecated since 4.10), so the arm builds a `GtkEntry` with a popover of items, or answers unsupported with words

capability ui/progress  a bar filled as far as a value goes
  heroes  ui.progress, ui.set_value, ui.value_of, ui.set_range
